.TH PRINTF 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
printf \- format and print data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B printf
.I format
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printf
writes
.I format
to standard output, replacing each conversion specification with the
next
.I argument
formatted accordingly.
There are no options; the first operand is always the format.
.PP
Backslash escapes in
.I format
are interpreted first:
.B \e\e
(backslash),
.B \ea
(alert),
.B \eb
(backspace),
.B \ee
(escape),
.B \ef
(form feed),
.B \en
(newline),
.B \er
(carriage return),
.B \et
(tab),
.B \ev
(vertical tab),
.BI \e nnn
(the byte with octal value
.IR nnn ,
one to three digits) and
.BI \ex HH
(the byte with hexadecimal value
.IR HH ,
one or two digits).
A backslash followed by any other character is printed unchanged,
together with that character.
.PP
A conversion specification is
.B %
followed by optional flags
.RB ( \- ,
.BR + ,
space,
.BR # ,
.BR 0 ),
an optional field width, an optional precision
.RB ( .
followed by digits), and a conversion character.
The width and the precision may each be given as
.BR * ,
in which case they are taken from the next argument; a negative width
from an argument means left alignment, a negative precision is treated
as 0.
Widths and precisions are capped at 1048576.
.B %%
prints a percent sign.
.PP
The conversions are:
.TP
.BR d ", " i
Signed decimal integer.
.TP
.BR o ", " u ", " x ", " X
Unsigned octal, decimal and hexadecimal integers.
.TP
.BR e ", " E ", " f ", " F ", " g ", " G ", " a ", " A
Floating-point number, converted with
.BR strtod (3).
.TP
.B c
The first byte of the argument.
Nothing at all is printed for an empty or missing argument.
.TP
.B s
The argument as a string.
.TP
.B b
The argument as a string in which the backslash escapes listed above
(except
.BR \ee ,
.B \ex
and plain
.BR \e nnn)
are interpreted; an octal value is written as
.B \e0
followed by up to three octal digits.
.B \ec
in the argument stops all output immediately, and
.B printf
exits.
Precision limits the number of bytes written; width pads with spaces.
.PP
Integer arguments are converted as C
.B long
values, in decimal, octal (leading
.BR 0 )
or hexadecimal (leading
.BR 0x ).
An argument that starts with a single or double quote converts to the
byte value of the character after the quote.
An argument that is not entirely a valid number, or is out of range,
is diagnosed on standard error and the converted value is used anyway;
the exit status becomes 1.
Missing arguments are treated as 0 for numeric conversions and as the
empty string otherwise.
.PP
If arguments remain after the format has been used, the format is
reused from the beginning until all arguments are consumed.
A pass over the format that consumes no argument ends the output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
No
.I format
was given, an argument could not be fully converted, an invalid
conversion character was used, or memory ran out.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ printf \(aq%s is %d years old\en\(aq Ann 34
Ann is 34 years old
$ printf \(aq%\-5s|%05.1f\en\(aq a 3.14159 b 2
a    |003.1
b    |002.0
$ printf \(aq%d\en\(aq "\(aqA"
65
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR printf (3),
.BR strtod (3)
.SH STANDARDS
Intended to follow POSIX.1-2024
.BR printf ,
with the
.BR \ee ,
.B \ex
and floating-point conversions as extensions.
.SH BUGS
Escapes in
.I format
are expanded before conversion specifications are recognised, so an
escape that produces
.B %
(for example
.BR \e045 )
starts a conversion instead of printing a literal percent sign, and
.B \e0
ends the format.
.PP
.B \ec
is not recognised in
.IR format ,
only in
.B %b
arguments.
.PP
A leading
.B \-\-
is not skipped; it is used as the format.
